'''8.''' In a position of retirement or withdrawal; off; aloof: absolutely or with ''from'': as, the house stands a little ''back from'' the road.
{{smaller block|Somewhat ''back from'' the village street
Stands the old-fashioned country-seat.
{{right|''Longfellow'', Old Clock.}}}}
'''9.''' Behind in position, literally or figuratively, or as regards progress made: absolutely or with ''of'': as, the hills ''back of'' the town; the feeling ''back of'' his words; a few pages ''back''.—'''10.''' Past in time; ago; since: as, a little ''back''. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|This precaution, still more salutary than offensive, has for some years ''back'' been omitted.
{{right|Quoted in ''N. and Q.'', 7th ser., II. 106.}}}}
'''11.''' Again; in return: as, to answer ''back''; to pay ''back'' a loan.
{{smaller block|“Ruth — daughter Ruth!” the outlaw shrieks,
But no sound comes ''back'' — he is standing alone.
{{right|''Whittier'', Mogg Megone, i.}}}}
'''To and back†''', {{smaller|forward and backward; to and fro.}}
{{smaller block|This common body,
Like to a vagabond flag upon the stream,
Goes ''to and back'', lackeying the varying tide.
{{right|''Shak.'', A. and C., i. 4.}}}}
'''To beat, draw, fall, hang,''' {{smaller|etc.,}} '''back.''' {{smaller|See the verbs.}}

'''back<sup>1</sup>''' (bak), ''v.'' [In senses I., 1–8, < ''back''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''; in senses I., 9–11, and II., < ''back''<sup>1</sup>, ''adv.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To furnish with a back or backing; strengthen or support at the back: as, to ''back'' a book; to ''back'' an electrotype-plate; to ''back'' the armor-plates of a war-vessel with teak.—'''2†.''' To cover the back of; clothe.
{{smaller block|To breke beggeris bred and ''bakken'' hem with clothis.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), xi. 185.}}}}

