'''3.''' To support or aid, as with practical assistance, money, authority, influence, etc.; second or strengthen; reinforce: often with ''up'': as, in his efforts he was ''backed'' by many influential men; he ''backed up'' his argument with a bet.
{{smaller block|<poem>Success still follows him and ''backs'' his crimes.</poem>
{{right|''Addison.''}}}}
{{smaller block|The men of the northern Danelaw found themselves ''backed'', not only by their brethren from Ireland, but by the mass of states around them.
{{right|''J. R. Green'', Conq. of Eng., p. 243.}}}}
Hence—'''4.''' In ''sporting'', to recognize and support by standing or dropping: said of dogs which follow the lead of a dog on point.
{{smaller block|Both dogs went off finely; soon after being put down Foreman pointed and was ''backed'' by Gath.
{{right|''Forest and Stream'', XXI. 418.}}}}
'''5.''' To act or wager in favor of; express confidence in the success or superiority of: as, to ''back'' a horse in a race, or one of the parties in an argument.
{{smaller block|I ''back'' him at a rebus or a charade against the best rhymer in the kingdom.
{{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, i. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|They [Bedouins] are fond of ''backing'' themselves with wagers, and will shoot for a sheep, the loser inviting his friends to a feast. ''R. F. Burton'', El-Medinah, p. 336.}}
'''6.''' To get upon the back of; mount: as, to ''back'' a horse.
{{smaller block|<poem>We both will ''back'' the winds,
And hunt the phœnix through the Arabian deserts.</poem>
{{right|''Shirley'', Grateful Servant, iv. 5.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And he has reached the northern plain,
And ''backed'' his fire-fly steed again.</poem>
{{right|''J. R. Drake'', Culprit Fay, p. 59.}}}}
